# N4-C: the interface is placed over the real render surface, and the application survives leaving the
# foreground with nothing latched.
#
# Two different claims, two different kinds of evidence.
#
# Placement and the Ballpad header are geometry, and geometry here is SunPad's own: the vendored bytes
# are unchanged and the only adaptation is a UIMenu re-wrapped under a Ballpad title, so the honest
# evidence is a Simulator screenshot rather than a rule in this file.  What this scenario proves is
# that the interface is actually attached to the window the port renders into -- the adapter prints
# the frame it got and the window it attached to, from inside `PortHostUIStart`.
#
# Lifecycle is behaviour and can be driven.  Doc 34 F07 asks for five background/foreground cycles, and
# no in-process action can send an application to the background, so the driver grew a `host` directive:
# it brings another application to the front, which backgrounds this one, and then brings this one back.
# Coming back is a launch of an already-running process, so it activates rather than relaunches -- a
# resume, which is the case being tested; the PID staying put is what tells the two apart.
#
# `dump` is the frame-loop witness and it is why one follows every foreground.  A control line is only
# acknowledged from inside the frame loop, so a `dump` that returns can only have returned because a
# frame ran to read the control file.  The ack carries that frame, so the five cycles produce five
# increasing frames after five silences -- a resume, not a frozen process still holding a socket.  A
# `require-frame` could not make that argument: it resolves off periodic engine lines and says nothing
# about who was still running.
#
# STRIKERS_LOG_INPUT prints the pad's edge state after every resample, which is how a stuck button is
# told apart from a benign one: after each resume the published pad must read neutral (`cur=0x0000` for
# pad 0).  Nothing in this scenario holds a port-channel button across a cycle on purpose -- a hold
# there is frame-counted, so it would correctly still be held on the far side and would only make the
# neutral readings harder to read.
env STRIKERS_LOG_SCENES=1
env STRIKERS_LOG_INPUT=1
require-frame 180
expect [SunPad] host ui: overlay
expect [SunPad] host ui: first pad poll
dump before_cycles
host background
host foreground
dump cycle1
host background
host foreground
dump cycle2
host background
host foreground
dump cycle3
host background
host foreground
dump cycle4
host background
host foreground
dump cycle5
shot after_cycles
dump after_cycles
expect [SunPad] host ui: background;
expect [SunPad] host ui: foreground;
expect [SunPad] host ui: active;
